t_revision_alternatives (rs : List Record) (r s : Record) : Prop :=\n  alternatives_at_greatest rs r s\n\n/- Possible reservations per booking. -/\ndef possible_reservations_for (snap : Snapshot) (b : Booking) : List Quantity :=\n  possible_reservations snap b\n\n/- Availability bounds. -/\ndef lower_availability (snap : Snapshot) (bookings : List Booking) : Int :=\n  availability_lower_bound snap bookings\n\ndef upper_availability (snap : Snapshot) (bookings : List Booking) : Int :=\n  availability_upper_bound snap bookings\n\n/- Conjectures, commitments, refutations. -/\ndef SourceConjecture : Type := Conjecture\ndef SourceCommitment : Type := Commitment\ndef SourceRefutation (c : Conjecture) : Type := Refutation c\n\n/- Rule disagreement. -/\ndef source_rules_disagree (r1 r2 : ReservationRule) (snap : Snapshot) : Prop :=\n  rules_disagree r1 r2 snap\n\n/- Alternative rule: expiry deletes the record, so a lower revision can become greatest. -/\ndef source_expired_deletes_rule : ReservationRule :=\n  expired_deletes_rule\n\n/- Deletion can change which record is greatest. -/\ndef source_deletion_changes_greatest (snap : Snapshot) : Prop :=\n  deletion_changes_greatest snap\n\nend WarehouseSnapshotSource\n```\n\n## PROSE-ESCAPE\n\nThe following exact source fragments are substantive but cannot be carried by WHL without adding primitives or changing the source. They are retained here as `PROSE-ESCAPE`.\n\n**PROSE-ESCAPE:**\n> \"A replicated warehouse snapshot is not a single ledger but a cloud of rival stories about what has been promised. The capacity and logical time are shared coordinates, but the records arrive as a bag: orderless, possibly repeated, possibly stale, possibly contradictory. The first useful conjecture is that the snapshot's job is not to compute one timeless answer but to decide which conflicts are still alive. Duplicate delivery of the same booking, revision, kind, quantity, and expiry adds nothing; authority does not accumulate by repetition. A higher revision kills lower revisions for that booking, but only within the booking. Two bookings can disagree about which revision they have accepted without infecting each other.\"\n\nObstruction: WHL can carry the capacity/logical-time/record-list skeleton and the within-booking comparison via `higher_revision`, but it has no primitives for \"rival stories\", \"promised\", \"stale\", \"contradictory\", \"snapshot's job\", \"conflicts alive\", \"authority\", or \"infecting\".\n\n**PROSE-ESCAPE:**\n> \"For any one booking, the natural reconstruction is to group its records by revision, then keep only the greatest revision present. Within that greatest revision, a hold and a release cannot both be in force as the same fact; they are alternatives. An expired hold and a release both reserve zero. An active hold reserves its quantity. If several active holds tie at the greatest revision, they remain unresolved alternatives, not a sum and not a vote. That is a deliberate refusal: the rules do not say how to merge them, so the snapshot should retain the possibility that any one is the true one, or that none is, depending on what \\\"true\\\" is taken to mean.\"\n\nObstruction: WHL can carry `greatest_revision_in`, `alternatives_at_greatest`, and `reserves_effect`, but it has no \"in force as the same fact\", \"unresolved\", \"deliberate refusal\", \"merge\", \"true\", or \"possibility\".\n\n**PROSE-ESCAPE:**\n> \"Availability is then capacity minus whatever reservation is actually selected, but the word \\\"actually\\\" hides the problem. If each booking's greatest revision is unambiguous, availability may still be negative if the selected holds exceed capacity. Negative availability is not an error in the arithmetic; it is a signal that the warehouse has over-promised relative to this reconstruction. If different bookings have unresolved alternatives, availability is not a number but a set or an interval, and possibly not even a convex one. A pessimistic reading takes the maximum hold among alternatives for each booking; an optimistic reading takes the minimum. Neither is forced by the snapshot alone.\"\n\nObstruction: WHL can carry negative availability via `Int.sub_nat` and interval bounds via `availability_lower_bound`/`availability_upper_bound`, but it has no \"actually selected\", \"signal\", \"over-promised\", \"set\", \"convex\", \"pessimistic/optimistic reading\", or \"forced\".\n\n**PROSE-ESCAPE:**\n> \"Now the disagreements begin.\"\n\nObstruction: WHL has no primitive for \"disagreement\" as a first-class object.\n\n**PROSE-ESCAPE:**\n> \"One disagreement is whether uncertainty should be retained at all. A reconciler might collapse each tie to the largest quantity, or to the earliest expiry, or to the record with some lexical tiebreak, and thereby produce a single availability number. That is convenient, but it manufactures authority. Another reconciler might keep all alternatives and report a range. That preserves honesty but makes downstream decisions harder. The snapshot description does not settle which is correct; it only says tied records can remain unresolved. A conjecture: the more useful representation is a partial order of possible reservations, not a set, because some alternatives may be compatible with others across bookings while others are not. A criticism: once you move to a set of possible worlds, you have smuggled in a notion of possibility that the original bag did not contain.\"\n\nObstruction: WHL has `ReservationRule` and `possible_reservations`, but no \"uncertainty retained\", \"reconciler\", \"lexical tiebreak\", \"authority\", \"honesty\", \"downstream decisions\", \"partial order of possible reservations\", \"compatible\", \"possible worlds\", or \"smuggled\".\n\n**PROSE-ESCAPE:**\n> \"A second disagreement concerns expiry and obsolete records. If logical current time is past a hold's expiry, that hold reserves zero. But does expiry erase the record, demote it, or merely change its effect? Suppose a booking has revision 5 expired and revision 3 active. Higher revision supersedes lower revision, so revision 5 wins, and because it is expired it reserves zero. Revision 3 is obsolete and should not be resurrected. That reading treats revision as authority and expiry as effect. Another reading treats expiry as deleting the record from the bag, after which revision 3 becomes greatest and active, so the booking reserves again. These give opposite answers. The snapshot description tilts toward the first: a higher revision supersedes lower revisions, and expiry can make a hold reserve zero. It does not say expiry removes the supersession. So the first is better supported, but the second remains a live conjecture, especially if one thinks expired records are no longer \\\"delivered records\\\" in the relevant sense. A criticism of the first: it lets a dead revision keep a live one buried forever, which may be undesirable operationally.\"\n\nObstruction: WHL can carry the mechanical reading with `greatest_revision_in` and `reserves_effect`, and it has `expired_deletes_rule` and `deletion_changes_greatest` for the alternative, but it has no \"obsolete\", \"erase\", \"demote\", \"resurrected\", \"authority\", \"effect\", \"tilts toward\", \"better supported\", \"live conjecture\", \"delivered records\", \"dead/live\", \"buried\", or \"operationally undesirable\". Also `expired_deletes_rule` does not actually delete from the list before `greatest_revision_in`.\n\n**PROSE-ESCAPE:**\n> \"A third disagreement is what a criticism targets. One can criticize a reconstruction rule, a particular record, a booking's history, the snapshot as a whole, or the assumption that logical time is meaningful. For example, saying \\\"this snapshot is inconsistent\\\" might mean two active holds for one booking at the same greatest revision, or it might mean total reservations exceed capacity, or it might mean the logical time is behind an expiry that someone expected to have passed. These