vailability can be negative, which suggests over-reservation is allowed. But negative availability does not say which holds are the offenders. If there are alternatives, some combinations may be negative and others not. A conjecture: the sign of availability is less important than the set of reservations that produce it, because a negative number alone does not tell you which booking to revisit. A criticism: if availability is allowed to be negative, then capacity is not a hard constraint, and the word \\\"capacity\\\" may be misleading; it is more like a soft target or a reporting threshold.\"\n\nObstruction: WHL can compute negative availability via `Int.sub_nat`, but it has no \"inconsistency\", \"reject\", \"valid state\", \"over-reservation\", \"offenders\", \"set of reservations\", \"sign importance\", \"hard constraint\", \"soft target\", or \"reporting threshold\".\n\n**PROSE-ESCAPE:**\n> \"A seventh disagreement is about revision gaps and missing records. Suppose a booking has revision 7 and revision 5, but no revision 6. Does revision 7 supersede revision 5? Yes, if higher revision supersedes lower. Does the absence of revision 6 matter? Not by the stated rules, but it might indicate a lost delivery. A conjecture: revision numbers are not necessarily contiguous, and treating a gap as evidence of loss is an extra assumption. A criticism: if revisions are generated in order, a gap may be meaningful, and ignoring it may hide a delivery failure.\"\n\nObstruction: WHL has `higher_revision` and `revision_lt`, so it can express supersession, but it has no contiguity, gap, lost delivery, evidence of loss, extra assumption, generated in order, or delivery failure.\n\n**PROSE-ESCAPE:**\n> \"An eighth disagreement is about tie-breaking across bookings. Different bookings need not share the same choice, so one cannot impose a global tiebreak. But a downstream consumer might want a single availability number. To get one, it must choose per booking, possibly arbitrarily